<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Shapes Around Us – Lesson # 2</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 20px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 1st Grade (CEFR: Pre-A1.3)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Shapes Around Us</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> I See a Rectangle.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Reading: Recognize familiar words in simple texts. Reading: Match simple words to corresponding pictures. Phonemic Awareness: Identify sounds of the letters of the alphabet when supported with a concrete item or picture representing that sound.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can point to pictures of shapes when prompted during reading activities (e.g., pointing to a picture of a triangle in a storybook when asked, "What is this shape?"). Can match simple shape-related words to pictures (e.g., "square," "circle"). Can produce the sound of the alphabet letters when supported by a concrete item or picture representing the sound (e.g., /s/ in "square").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center; margin-top: 30px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; text-align: left; width: 40%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; text-align: left; width: 45%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; text-align: left; width: 15%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification of the lesson's core content, ensuring students are ready for the main task.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher starts by displaying large shape flashcards (square, rectangle, circle, triangle, oval, diamond, star) from the previous lesson around the room. The teacher plays a "Shape Song" and encourages students to point to the shapes as they hear them mentioned. Then, the teacher holds up one flashcard at a time and asks, "What shape is it?" prompting students to recall the names. (Vocabulary: square, rectangle, circle, triangle, oval, diamond, star; Grammar: What shape is it?)</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up a flashcard with the word "square" and a picture of a square. The teacher explicitly points to the word "square" and says the word, emphasizing the /s/ sound, "This is a <b>s</b>quare." Then, the teacher points to the word "rectangle" and says, "This is a <b>r</b>ectangle," emphasizing the /r/ sound. The teacher demonstrates how to slowly trace the word with a finger while saying it. (Pronunciation: /s/ in "square," /r/ in "rectangle"; Phonemic Awareness: initial sounds in names of shapes).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher uses simple questions and gestures to check understanding of the words and their sounds.</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Pointing to the word "circle" on a flashcard) "Is this a square?" (Expected answer: "No," or shake head).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Pointing to the word "rectangle") "What sound does this word start with? /s/ or /r/?" (Expected answer: "/r/").</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">8 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Introduction of new language/content through authentic input, connected to the scenario "Shapes Around Us" and theme "I See a Rectangle."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A large, simple illustrated storybook or a Big Book with repetitive sentences about shapes found in everyday objects (e.g., "I see a rectangle. It is a door. The door is tall. That is a square. It is a window. The window is small."). The text includes the target vocabulary (square, rectangle, circle, big, small, tall, long, short) and grammar (That is a circle; Shape + noun: a square table; WH-questions: What shape is it?).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Identifying shapes: Recognize and name shapes like squares and circles during classroom activities; Describing shapes: Use simple phrases to talk about shapes (e.g., "This is a rectangle.").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher reads the story aloud, pointing to each word as they read. Students follow along, pointing with their fingers.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">After reading each page, the teacher asks "What shape is it?" and students point to the shape in the picture and to the corresponding word in the text.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher asks students to "Find the word 'square'!" and students try to point to the word in the text.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher holds up a flashcard (e.g., "rectangle") and asks students to find the word in the storybook and point to it.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">12 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Focus on accuracy in reading, practicing target language in controlled and semi-controlled activities.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Reading: Recognizing familiar words in simple texts, matching simple words to pictures. Grammar: Shape + noun (e.g., "a square table"). Vocabulary: square, rectangle, circle, triangle, oval, diamond, star, see, look, find, be (is), big, small, long, short, tall, round.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Word-Picture Matching:</b> Students receive a worksheet with pictures of shapes (square, rectangle, circle, triangle, oval) on one side and the corresponding shape words mixed up on the other. Students draw a line from each picture to its correct word. The teacher circulates, providing support and checking for correct matches. (Specific Objective: Match simple words to corresponding pictures).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Listen and Circle the Word:</b> The teacher calls out a shape word (e.g., "square," "rectangle," "circle"). Students have a worksheet with rows of simple sentences or words. For example: "I see a <u>square</u>. This is a <u>circle</u>. Look, a <u>rectangle</u>." Students listen to the teacher and circle the word they hear. This activity reinforces phonemic awareness and word recognition. (Specific Objective: Recognize familiar words in simple texts; Phonemic Awareness: Identify sounds).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Shape Hunt and Read (Pair Check):</b> Students work in pairs. Each pair gets a small set of shape word flashcards (e.g., 'square', 'rectangle'). The teacher instructs students to "Look for a square table!" One student points to a square object in the classroom, and the other student *reads* the word 'square' from their flashcard. They then switch roles. This encourages reading the word and connecting it to real objects. (Pragmatic: Identifying shapes; Sociolinguistic: Describing characteristics of objects).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students use the language in a realistic communicative task, focusing on fluency and task completion within the scenario "Shapes Around Us."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> "My Shape Book" creation and reading. Students will create a very simple, personalized mini-book about shapes they see, focusing on reading the words they write/draw.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher provides each student with a pre-folded mini-book template (4-6 pages). Each page has a blank space for drawing and a sentence stem like "I see a _____." or "It is a _____."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students are instructed to draw an object of a specific shape on each page (e.g., a square window, a round sun, a rectangular door).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">After drawing, students carefully copy the corresponding shape word from word cards (provided by the teacher) into the blank in their sentence stem.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Once complete, students take turns "reading" their mini-books to a partner or the teacher, pointing to the words and pictures. They say, "I see a [shape]. It is [adjective]." (e.g., "I see a square. It is big."). (Grammar: Present simple; Shape + noun. Vocabulary: see, is, big, small, long, short, tall, round).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment of student learning and a brief self-reflection opportunity.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during "My Shape Book" activity, focusing on word recognition and matching.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students will individually complete an "Exit Ticket" worksheet. The worksheet will have 3-4 pictures of shapes (e.g., a square, a circle, a rectangle). Below each picture, there will be two shape words, one correct and one incorrect (e.g., picture of a square with words "square" and "circle" below it). Students circle the correct word for each picture. (Learning Outcome: Can match simple shape-related words to pictures).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Accurate circling of shape words on the exit ticket (matching words to pictures).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to point to familiar words when reading their mini-book.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Production of initial sounds of shape names when prompted.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can find the word 'square'." (Students draw a happy face or a sad face next to it).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can match a word to a picture." (Students draw a happy face or a sad face next to it).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">6 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Review of the lesson's effectiveness and planning for future lessons.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection Prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new shape word can you read today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was your favorite shape to read about?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The picture-word matching and "Listen and Circle" activities were very effective for reinforcing word recognition for 1st graders. The "My Shape Book" was engaging, but some students needed more scaffolding for copying words accurately.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Next lesson (Speaking) will focus on orally describing shapes and objects using the target vocabulary. I will prepare more visuals and provide sentence frames to support speaking practice, building on the reading foundation established today.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> Students will use the shape words they recognized and read today to actively speak and describe objects in Lesson 3 (Speaking & Interaction).</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">4 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>